course: politics
lesson: 30
title: Refugees, aid and sanctions: how the world helps and pushes
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A refugee had to flee war or danger to their life; a migrant moves to another country, often for work or a better life. A 1951 agreement says countries must not send refugees back to danger. The word you choose changes how people see the same family.
explain: Imagine your street suddenly unsafe: some neighbours reach a camp just over the border, some pay smugglers to cross the sea in small boats, some are welcomed into a stranger's spare room. All of these happen in today's world.
explain: Countries help with aid — food, water and medicine, mostly carried by trucks — and push with sanctions, refusing trade or freezing money to make a government change course. Each tool has supporters and critics, and the question «does it work?» always needs «at what?».
explain: Helpers like the Red Cross and Red Crescent stay neutral so they can reach every side. Ordinary people help too, and the smartest giving asks the helpers what they need and checks that a charity is real.
explain: Words from the scenes: «yer man» and «fella» (Ireland) = that man, «the craic» = what's going on; «end of» = no more arguing; «wee» (Belfast, Scotland) = small; «hella» (California) = very; «jandals» (New Zealand) = flip-flops; «bloody» = a mild swear word.
fact: A refugee is someone who fled war or danger to their life; a migrant moves to another country, often for work or a better life.
fact: The 1951 Refugee Convention, an international agreement, says countries must not send refugees back to danger.
fact: After Russia's full-scale invasion in 2022, millions of Ukrainians fled, and the UK's Homes for Ukraine scheme let local families host them.
fact: Refugee camps, such as those for Rohingya people in Bangladesh, can hold hundreds of thousands of people for years; the UN refugee agency, UNHCR, runs or supports many of them.
fact: People pay smugglers to cross the English Channel in small, unsafe boats, and some have drowned; governments argue about how to stop the crossings and how to treat those who arrive.
fact: The Red Cross and Red Crescent help people in wars and disasters without taking sides, visit prisoners of war and help families find missing relatives; attacking them breaks the rules of war, the Geneva Conventions.
fact: Several countries dropped food from planes over Gaza when trucks could not bring in enough; aid groups said airdrops carry far less than trucks, cost more and can be dangerous for people below.
fact: Sanctions are when countries refuse to trade with, sell to or lend money to a country, company or person to push them to change; many countries put sanctions on Russia after 2022 and froze money belonging to its central bank.
fact: News reports said sanctions made life harder for Russia's economy, but Russia found other buyers for its oil, so they hurt but did not stop the war.
fact: Charities often prefer money to tins and clothes, because they can buy what is needed near the crisis faster and more cheaply.
fact: News reports said that in 2025 the US cut much of its foreign aid and the UK reduced its aid to spend more on defence; aid groups warned that programmes would close, and governments said the money was needed at home or for security.
fact: Some online appeals with crying children and payment links are scams; real charities are registered and can be checked on an official charity register.
exercise: What is a refugee? | Someone who fled war or danger to their life | Someone who moved abroad for a better-paid job | A tourist on a very long holiday, with a return ticket | A soldier sent abroad to fight in somebody else's war
exercise: What is a migrant? | Someone who moves to another country, often for work or a better life | Someone who has never left their home town | A bird that only flies at night | A UN peacekeeper on duty
exercise: What does the 1951 Refugee Convention say? | Countries must not send refugees back to danger | Every refugee must go home within a week | Refugee children may not go to school | Only rich people can be refugees
exercise: Where does Sofiia from Kharkiv live in Brighton? | With a local family who offered their spare room | In a hotel on the Moon | Alone in a tent on the beach | In her old flat in Kharkiv
exercise: What can be true of some refugee camps? | Some children have lived in them their whole lives | They are only open for one night | They are holiday resorts | They are built inside London schools
exercise: What is UNHCR? | The UN agency that looks after refugees | The group that runs the Olympic Games every four years | A company that builds warships for navies | A factory that makes phone chips in Taiwan
exercise: Why are small-boat crossings of the Channel dangerous? | The boats are small and unsafe, and some people drown | The Channel has no water in it at low tide, so people walk | Boats are only allowed in the Channel in the summer months | The boats are far too big for the sea and can't turn round
exercise: What do the two sides say about the small boats? | Some say stop them because they are dangerous and help gangs; others say people need safe legal routes | Everyone agrees the boats are fine | Nobody ever talks about the boats | Both sides want more smugglers, because smugglers bring in the workers that every country needs the most
exercise: What do the red cross and red crescent symbols on a truck mean? | Neutral helpers in wars and disasters, who must not be attacked | Trucks that belong to one army only, carrying its wounded soldiers back home from the front | The flags of two football clubs | A sign that the truck carries weapons
exercise: Why are airdrops not the main way to get food into Gaza? | They carry far less than trucks, cost more and can be dangerous below | Planes cannot fly over the sea | Food cannot be packed in boxes | People prefer food that falls from the sky, because it feels like a present from far away
exercise: What are sanctions? | Refusing trade or freezing money to push a country to change | Sending soldiers to fight | Giving a country free food | A kind of peace treaty
exercise: Ruby's friend says sanctions are like the class not swapping cards with a cheat. What is one difference? | Sanctions are official rules by governments and can hurt ordinary people too | Sanctions only ever affect one child | Sanctions are a game with no effect | Sanctions mean giving the cheat more cards, so that he has no reason left to cheat ever again
exercise: Why do experts disagree about whether sanctions on Russia work? | They hurt Russia's economy but did not stop the war, so it depends on the goal | Nobody has ever measured anything about Russia's economy, so every expert is only guessing | Sanctions were never used on Russia | Russia stopped the war at once
exercise: Why do charities often prefer money to tins and old clothes? | They can buy what is needed near the crisis, faster and cheaper | Tins are banned by the UN | Money weighs more than tins | They want to keep the money themselves
exercise: What did news reports say about foreign aid in 2025? | The US cut much of it and the UK reduced aid to spend more on defence | Every country doubled its aid, and the US gave more than all the others put together | Foreign aid was invented that year | Aid was made illegal everywhere
exercise: How can Priya check that an online appeal is real? | Check the charity on an official register with a grown-up | Send money fast, before the link disappears and it is too late | Trust it, because the child in the picture is really crying | Share the link with all her friends first, and ask them to check
test: Liam in Dublin asks: «yer man on the radio says refugee, the other fella says migrant, same family — what's the craic?» What is the best answer? | The right word depends on why they left; fleeing danger makes someone a refugee | Both words mean exactly the same in law, so the radio men are just using the one they like best | Refugee means rich and migrant means poor | Neither word has any meaning
test: A family flees bombing and reaches a new country. What would the 1951 Refugee Convention forbid that country to do? | Send them back to the danger | Let their children go to school | Let them find work and pay taxes | Give them a place to sleep at night
test: Jake's grandad in Kent barks: «stop the bloody boats, end of.» His teacher says: «they're desperate people.» Which sentence keeps both sides' best case? | The crossings are dangerous and help gangs, and many on board fled danger and need safe routes | The boats are perfectly safe | Nobody on the boats was ever in danger, so they should all be sent straight back across the Channel | Everyone agrees on what to do
test: Oisin in Belfast asks: «is that a wee hospital truck, the one with the red crescent?» What is the best answer? | It is the Red Crescent, a neutral helper like the Red Cross, used in many Muslim countries | It is an army truck carrying weapons, painted to look like a hospital so that nobody shoots at it | It is a pizza delivery van | It belongs to one side of the war only
test: Mateo in Los Angeles says: «planes dropping food on Gaza, hella cool, problem solved.» Which reasoning is best? | Airdrops help a little but carry far less than trucks, so opening roads matters most | Airdrops carry more than any number of trucks | Food from planes is always safer than food from trucks, because planes can't be stopped on the road | Trucks are never used for aid
test: Ruby's friend compares sanctions to the class refusing to swap cards with a cheat. Where does the comparison break down? | In a country, ordinary people who did nothing wrong may also pay the cost | It is exactly the same in every way, because a country is just a very big class with a leader | Sanctions only affect trading cards | Countries cannot trade anything at all
test: One expert says sanctions on Russia are «working» and another says they «failed». What is the clever question to ask? | «Working at what — weakening the economy, or ending the war?» | «Who has the louder voice?» | «Which expert is older?» | «Were they both wearing suits?»
test: Mia's school in Nelson ships 400 tins; a charity could buy the same food near the crisis. What is likely, as charities say? | Buying near the crisis is faster and cheaper, which is why money helps more | Shipped tins always arrive first, because ships are the fastest way to move food around the world | Tins cost less to fly than to buy nearby | Charities cannot buy food anywhere
test: Dylan's dad says «about time» and his mum says «people will die» about the aid cuts. Which statement fairly gives both views? | Some say spend on your own people first; others say aid saves lives for little money | Both want all aid ended | Both agree all aid is wasted, and only disagree about whether to spend it on roads or on schools | Neither of them cares about money
test: Priya sees a TikTok of a crying child with «send money now» and a link. What is the smartest first step? | Ask a grown-up to check the charity on an official register | Send the birthday money at once | Send money to every link she sees, just in case one of them is real and the child needs it | Reply with her parents' bank details
test: A boy in Rotorua jokes: «kids grew up in a camp? bet they love it, free jandals and all.» What is the truer picture? | A camp is meant as a short safe stop; growing up there means years without a real home | Camps are holiday parks that children choose, with pools, games and free jandals for everyone who stays | Every camp closes after a week | Camps are only for grown-ups
test: If the Red Cross openly took one side in a war, what would most likely happen? | The other side might stop letting it in to help people | Both sides would let it in more often | It would win the war | Nothing at all would change
exam: Identify the distinction between a refugee and a migrant. | A refugee has fled war or danger to life, whereas a migrant relocates, frequently for employment | A refugee relocates for employment, whereas a migrant has fled war or persecution and may not be returned | The two terms are legally identical in all respects | A refugee is a citizen of the United Nations
exam: State the principal obligation imposed by the Refugee Convention of 1951. | States shall not return refugees to territories in which they face danger | States shall return all refugees to their country of origin within seven days | States shall accept no refugees whatsoever, irrespective of the danger faced | States shall convey refugees by air only, at their own expense, to a third state
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reported effect of sanctions upon Russia? | They impaired the Russian economy, whereas they did not bring the war to an end | They terminated the war within a single week | They exerted no effect whatsoever upon the Russian economy, which grew more swiftly than in any previous year | They augmented Russian revenues from all sources
exam: Select the response consistent with the established reasons for which charities frequently prefer monetary donations. | Funds permit the purchase of required goods near the crisis more swiftly and cheaply | Monetary donations are mandated by the Geneva Conventions for every charity operating in a zone of conflict | Tinned goods are prohibited in all zones of conflict | Charities are forbidden to accept anything but coins
